WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:12.498
Hi! You can count apples one by one, but can you count water? Today we'll learn countable and uncountable nouns, and how to use many, much, a, an and the.

2
00:00:12.648 --> 00:00:20.346
Nouns we can count one by one are countable, like an apple and two apples. They have singular and plural forms.

3
00:00:20.496 --> 00:00:27.090
Nouns we can't count one by one are uncountable, like water, rice, furniture and luggage.

4
00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:36.402
Information and advice are uncountable too. Uncountable nouns have no plural, and we don't use a or an with them.

5
00:00:36.552 --> 00:00:44.538
To say a large amount, use many with countable nouns, like many books, and much with uncountable nouns, like much water.

6
00:00:44.688 --> 00:00:55.866
For a small amount, use few or a few with countable nouns, and little or a little with uncountable ones. Few and little mean hardly any; a few and a little mean some.

7
00:00:56.016 --> 00:01:01.098
A lot of, some and any can be used with both kinds.

8
00:01:01.248 --> 00:01:11.874
For a and an, look at the first sound, not the first letter. Use a before a consonant sound: a dog, a university. University starts with a you sound.

9
00:01:12.024 --> 00:01:18.042
Use an before a vowel sound: an umbrella, an hour. The h in hour is silent.

10
00:01:18.192 --> 00:01:26.418
Use the for a specific thing, or one already mentioned. I saw a cat. The cat was black.

11
00:01:26.568 --> 00:01:31.050
Watch out! She gave me many advices is wrong!

12
00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:39.042
Advice is uncountable, so it has no s and can't go with many. Say: She gave me a lot of advice.

13
00:01:39.192 --> 00:01:45.546
Information, furniture and luggage work the same way: no s.

14
00:01:45.696 --> 00:01:55.146
Your turn! There isn't ___ milk left. Many or much? Pause and think.

15
00:01:55.296 --> 00:02:01.794
The answer is much, because milk is uncountable. Did you get it right?

16
00:02:01.944 --> 00:02:11.130
Another one! We waited for ___ hour. A or an? Pause and think.

17
00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:19.482
The answer is an, because the h in hour is silent, so it starts with a vowel sound. Did you get it right?

18
00:02:19.632 --> 00:02:29.082
Try this one! There isn't ___ milk left. Much or many? Pause and think.

19
00:02:29.232 --> 00:02:35.730
The answer is much, because milk is uncountable. Did you get it right?

20
00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:47.082
Last one! Only ___ pupils came, so the hall was almost empty. Few or a few? Pause and think.

21
00:02:47.232 --> 00:02:55.818
The answer is few. It means hardly any, which is why the hall was almost empty. Did you get it right?

22
00:02:55.968 --> 00:03:09.546
Remember: use many and few with countable nouns, much and little with uncountable nouns, which never take an s. For a and an, listen to the first sound. Use the for something specific or already mentioned.

23
00:03:09.696 --> 00:03:15.498
Look around the kitchen: which things are countable, and which aren't?

